    faqs: [
      { question: "What is the most popular media wall colour for a living room?", answer: "Grey and charcoal tones are the most commonly requested media wall colours, largely because they suit almost any existing sofa colour, flooring tone and lighting setup without the higher maintenance of black or the marking of white." },
      { question: "Do black media walls show dust and fingerprints?", answer: "Yes, black media walls show dust, fingerprints and lint more readily than mid-tone greys or natural wood veneer, especially in a satin or gloss finish. A matt finish and a household routine of a quick wipe-down help keep this manageable." },
      { question: "Should a media wall be lighter or darker than the rest of the room?", answer: "Either can work well; it depends on whether you want the wall to stand out as a feature or recede into the background. A darker wall than the surrounding decor tends to read as a bold feature, while a similar or lighter tone lets the wall blend in and lets other elements, like a sofa or rug, take the visual lead." },
      { question: "What paint finish is best for a media wall?", answer: "Matt or satin/eggshell are best for most media walls. Matt gives the truest, flattest colour and suits a bold feature shade, while satin is more wipeable and better suited to busy family rooms; high-gloss is generally avoided as it reflects screen glare and highlights dust and fingerprints." },
      { question: "Does the colour of a media wall affect how visible the TV is when it's off?", answer: "Yes, a black or dark media wall lets the television's bezel and dead screen blend into the wall around it, while a white or light media wall makes the switched-off screen stand out more clearly as a dark rectangle against the paler surface." },
      { question: "Can I match my media wall colour to my flooring?", answer: "Yes, and it is one of the most reliable ways to choose a colour: warm oak or walnut flooring generally pairs best with warm greys, tan or oak-veneer media walls, while cooler, paler flooring pairs better with cool greys or crisp whites." },
    ],
